 ],
 "literal": "This boy, her five-part hair, to grasp also, and this girl, his / sparse-haired head's single lock, she who pulls, to snap also, / loving foster-mothers, even when restraining, without stopping, / causeless little fights, they engage in indeed! / You are good indeed, oh fate! She of gentle nature, / and him, like a matching flower garland, these two, / their joy-of-marriage nature, you who showed!",
 "translation": "When this boy grabbed her five-part hair, and she pulled and snapped the single lock of hair on his sparse-haired head, their loving foster-mothers tried to restrain them, but without stopping, they engaged in causeless little fights. Oh fate, you are truly good! For you have now shown these two—she of gentle nature and him—united like a matching garland of flowers, in the joyful state of marriage.",
 "modern": "இந்தச் சிறுவன் இவளுடைய ஐந்து வகையாகப் பின்னப்பட்ட கூந்தலைப் பற்றவும், இவள் அவனுடைய மெல்லிய தலையிலுள்ள ஒற்றைக் குடுமியை இழுத்து அறுக்கவும், அன்புடைய செவிலித்தாயர் தடுத்தும் நில்லாமல், அவர்கள் காரணமில்லாத சிறு சண்டைகளைச் செய்து கொண்டிருப்பார்கள். விதியே! நீ மிகவும் நல்லவன். மென்மையான இயல்புடைய இவளையும் அவனையும், ஒன்றுக்கொன்று பொருத்தமான மலர்மாலை போல இணைத்து, அவர்கள் திருமண இன்பத்தில் மகிழ்ந்திருக்கும் இயற்கையான நிலையை எங்களுக்குக் காட்டியுள்ளாயே!",
 "explanation": "This poem is set in the Pālai (wasteland) landscape and is spoken by bystanders who witness a young couple who have eloped and are now happily married. The speakers recall the childhood of the boy and the girl, who used to fight constantly, grabbing each other's hair despite their foster-mothers' attempts to restrain them. These harmless, causeless childhood squabbles have now transformed into a deep, loving marital union. The bystanders praise fate (pāl) for its benevolence in bringing them together. The image of a matching flower garland (tuṇaimalarp piṇaiyal) beautifully illustrates their perfect compatibility and inseparable bond in marriage.",
